model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The account-owned observation credential is provisioned separately and stored directly in the deployment account's Secrets Store. Deployment binds only the stable secret name and never creates an API token, reads its value or depends on a person's connection. Provisioning requires positive account token-write authority and an explicit writer decision; an unknown create reply never authorizes another credential. Receipts retain only token identity and secret name. Application provisioning remains independently reviewed and cannot publish a Worker or change unrelated resources."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Operator fleet writes require an expiring writer GO whose digest covers the exact command, complete fleet configuration, controller code, destination and bridge key digest. The signed transport receives a fresh envelope of at most sixty seconds at dispatch, while the reviewed action and payload remain fixed. The private bridge sends once and closes on every outcome. The operator retains a durable submission marker before sending; repeated execution can only inspect that same operation, even after its writer GO expires. A lost acknowledgment is recovered by inspecting the same retained operation, including after the writer lease expires, without reissuing a mutation. Fleet economics depend on removing each Home node's idle database work across warming, standby and draining states; more ready nodes alone are not evidence of lower total cost."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet infrastructure is opt-in through two immutable same-account image pins. Blue and green have independent container namespaces, zero initial instances and provider maxima of four, while the single controller ledger limits all live and uncertain extra resources to four across both applications. The account-owned observation token has only container inventory read permission for that deployment account. A separately provisioned account Secrets Store secret is bound by stable name; deployment never creates an API token or reads its value. Missing or unreadable secrets produce unknown observation, prevent launch and retirement intent, and never prove absence. Each observation rereads the binding so provisioning restores observation without a Worker restart. Native global fetch is invoked without the reader as its receiver. Provider reads use workerd-compatible manual redirects and require status 200, so neither credentials nor observations follow a redirect. Provider transport exceptions retain only a closed provider_fetch_failed cause through signed operator refusal; exception messages, credentials and provider bodies never enter that response. An enabled fleet may resume a reserved launch only when its durable first-start intent is absent, proving compute was never dispatched; a retained intent permits observation only, and disabled permission cannot start compute. The root rechecks current fleet permission in the same storage transaction that commits first-start intent, fencing provider waits against an acknowledged disable. An ordinary release still refuses unadmitted creation or an update of a fleet application. Missing pins cannot delete previously declared infrastructure through the guarded release path. No image pin or declared binding grants permission to start compute."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Namespace provisioning preserves every existing module byte when the reviewed bundle already exports the target class. Proven absence permits a placeholder; wildcard ambiguity, duplicate exports and malformed code refuse rather than overwrite existing code. The namespace-first provisioning path accepts only the original singleton or the exact blue and green classes. Each fleet application starts with zero instances and a provider maximum of four; class, binding, resource and journal identities must agree. An independent reviewed manifest and expiring writer GO are required per application, with exact legacy namespace and active boot retention. Unknown application creation remains observation-only. Provisioning alone neither enables fleet growth nor expands the shared four-resource ledger guard. Stage root and endpoint observations read their own production records independently across DO stores; their coordinator joins the snapshots. These reads cannot probe, start, drain or change the retained lifecycle."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "During the first fleet transition, the retained legacy application uses its recorded immutable registry reference and original runtime input hash. A release still verifies the complete live provider baseline and image metadata before activation. Alchemy's reference-source hash stays separate from runtime provenance in the retained manifest. Changing the legacy image, configuration, namespace, placement or resource policy refuses preservation; a retained deletion policy alone is never a freeze. Fresh stages continue to build their own legacy image."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet launch permission is a signed, revision-checked deployment command naming both private application bindings, their provider configuration and the retained legacy boot. Provider refusal prevents activation. The resource reservation checks the current permission in the same transaction as its four-resource guard. Disabling permission stops growth and drains existing resources; unknown outcomes remain inspectable and never dispatch another launch. Planned replacement reserves its new resource before starting and retains the old ready runtime until the replacement is positively ready. The root serializes reservations with its durable fence, then waits for already reserved calls to reach their send boundary before dispatching endpoint drain through the same retained DO stub. Failed or cancelled calls release that barrier without replay. The endpoint serializes model admission with drain before non-storage health waits, so neither delayed headers nor active streams hold up the drain acknowledgment. Both durable counts must reach zero before retirement. A missing endpoint drain acknowledgment keeps maintenance due even while the root still holds active streams. Ordinary authorized model traffic balances execution across the primary and fleet; management and shared account admission retain their original authority. Private peer interception is installed only after the exact launch witness is retained, and peer requests cannot start or warm compute. The controller resolves source and target through its owned live rows, while endpoint checks bind the actual launch or retained legacy boot. Opaque peer bytes retain Home mutual TLS. Both upgrade-bearing hops use private stub fetch, because custom RPC cannot serialize a WebSocket response. RPC carries only peer-source and live-authority observations. Container-bound loopback props overwrite incoming source headers; the public Worker rejects the private peer transport path, and Root and endpoint recheck retained source, launch and boot authority before opening a socket. Fleet deadlines share the controller alarm without advancing Home collection. Primary lifecycle warmth records its Home deadline through the same scheduler, so an earlier fleet alarm cannot hide that deadline or consume later upkeep. Reconstruction preserves both deadlines and held primary request counts."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Runtime admission joins release-owned provider observations to a private trusted-start witness. A desired image binding or expected configuration hash is not evidence of the running image or its startup settings. The actual start callback retains the exact launch challenge and applied startup fingerprint before dispatch; authenticated readiness completes its boot. A failed witness write cannot start compute, and uncertain launch outcomes permit observation without a second start. Existing running compute may be preserved explicitly as unattested-active, never decoded as proven candidate evidence. Its adoption ACK and later passive readback derive the exact retained operation, manifest, decrypted capsule fingerprint, boot and committed selection from existing records. A fresh authenticated boot read and a selection recheck fence drift; capsule-only admission, another epoch, a candidate or draining slot, and an unknown ACK cannot authorize first-handoff advancement. Only a freshly joined attested candidate may pass promotion, with current controller, active selection and full positive and retired policy revisions still checked. The shared pool-runtime-attestation-v1 codec leaves existing slot and launch meanings unchanged. Private settings remain encrypted and stay out of receipts and logs. A manual main-only deployment credential audit reads token verification and self metadata without installing dependencies or making a provider write. Its output contains only status and permission-group names; unknown metadata does not establish token-creation permission. Ciphertext-corruption fixtures must change the decoded bytes and verify that precondition before expecting witness refusal; changing hex case alone is not corruption. Normal requests and explicit upkeep renew the selected runtime idle timer; synthetic probes and private health or witness reads never do. Legacy recovery cannot restart an admitted runtime, and initial adoption waits for any already admitted legacy restart to finish. Provider observations identify the exact named instance within a multi-instance application. Every active sibling must be running the same admitted image; an absent target, changed application settings or active rollout refuses admission. Instance-list ordering does not change observation identity. The new runtime class stays unbound until separately admitted provisioning and activation. A separate fleet ledger reserves before launch, counts warming and uncertain resources against the four-runtime guard, and retains held execution counts across reconstruction. Lost acknowledgements cannot dispatch a second launch or release capacity twice. Drain rejects delayed readiness; retired launch tombstones remain outside the bounded active-resource list. Logical cells isolate handoff journals while reading the existing canonical membership record, and cannot write that authority or start the legacy container. Scheduling state alone neither attests a runtime nor proves its endpoint has drained."
    }
  }
}
